A||||15||Shiner||Starting Problems||||5
Z||000000||Shiner||11-24-1999||04:51 PM||||Here is the story.  My fairly new '87 GT has some odd starting problems.  When it has been sitting for, say, overnight, it will start up on the first crank, but I have to give it some throttle.  If I JUST shut it down, like 3 minutes ago, it will start right up, again, with a little gas.  When I leave it sit for a few HOURS, it takes about 2.5 seconds of cranking and alot of gas to get her going.  It cranks good and hard, at about 500rpm.  I know my battery is not the problem, nor is the starter.  Lately, it has been taking even LONGER to start up.  What is up with this?  It runs great, and idles good at 900rpm.  What gives?  Oh yeah, I did just change the air filter, so it can't be that either.||||reg||5
Z||000001||2birds||11-24-1999||05:07 PM||||Do you hear the fuel pump run for a few seconds when you first take the key to the run position (before you start it)?  You should.  I've gone through two cheap fuel pump relays in the past 6 months.  The problem is intermittent for me.  Buy a Haynes manual and trace the schematic and you will see that the fuel pump relay gets turned on by a signal from the computer, to start the fuel pump to charge the injectors.  After the engine starts, the oil pressure switch maintains the circuit.  Post your results, though.  I may be going down the wrong path with mine.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Shiner||11-24-1999||11:58 PM||||My fuel pump fires up for two seconds when the ignition is set to the third position (on). I did try to start the car in diagnostic mode recently, and it smoked the fuel pump fuse.||||reg||2
Z||000003||Dan||11-25-1999||01:07 AM|||| It is not normal to smoke that fuse. I'd say its time to replace the fuel pump.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Shiner||11-25-1999||06:22 AM||||Ya think it's time for a new fuel pump, huh? But if the pump was bad, would it scream through the gears and just PURR at idle?! Once the engine is started up, she runs great.||||reg||9
Z||000005||William Federle||11-25-1999||07:04 AM||||Could it be a partially clogged fuel filter?||||reg||1
Z||000006||Shiner||11-26-1999||12:32 AM||||Okay, I'll try the fuel filter next.  I did'nt even think about that.  I'll let everybody know what happens.||||reg||8
Z||000007||2birds||11-26-1999||09:53 PM||||Shiner, et al<BR>I haven't replaced my fuel pump relay again yet, but I have just noticed a real hairy problem.  I was cruising along at about 70 mph tonight when it felt like the car just shut off and turned right back on again.  It did this a couple of times before I got to my exit, and then it stumbled again as I took off from the light.  I was like the car hiccupped or something.  I noticed an amber light for an instant at the bottom of my dash, and my tach went to about 3500 or so, so I'm going to check the diagnostics tomorrow and see if it set anything.  The only electrical thing I've done lately is replace the cap/rotor.  I had no fuel pump turn-on again when I went to start it, too.  Had to use starting fluid. <P>Any ideas?||||reg||1
Z||000008||2birds||11-26-1999||10:10 PM||||Two ideas I have, in addition to the fuel pump relay, are the ignition module overheating and failing(last replaced in 1995), and the pickup coil going bad(never messed with.  There was recently a post about the ignition module on the Central Florida Fieros BBS, but much of it was deleted, and the Fiero online service guide gave me the idea about the pickup coil.  What do you experts out there think, before I start card- swapping? ||||reg||1
Z||000009||kornholio||11-26-1999||10:30 PM||||Hey 2birds,<BR>If you can't find the problem, check your TPS.  The problem you described sounds like the problem I had 2 months ago.  It would only happen off and on.  And the service engine light didn't come on at all.||||reg||1
Z||000010||2birds||11-26-1999||10:56 PM||||I saw something about the TPS in the same Central FL Fieros BBS post, too, but most of the post was removed.  I last replaced mine at 105,000, 6 years ago.  Idled at around 2,000 rpm and set the trouble code, too.  The first one I tried was a borg-warner part from pep boys, and it didn't fix it.  Got an AC Delco one the next monday, and it worked like a champ.  That's why my next fuel pump relay will come from AC Delco.<BR>Thanks for the idea.||||reg||1
Z||000011||dupi||11-27-1999||01:32 AM||||This sounds like what I went through this summer with my 86GT right after I tunned it up.  I ended up replacing my ignition module and the ignition coil both parts went bad. But as I was replacing the ignition coil for the second time in two weeks, I noticed that the wire that conected the two parts had a soft spot near one of the conectors.  When I removed the heat shrink from the wire there was only one thin coppor strand maintraining the conection so I went to the local junk yard and found me a replacement(found being the key word). And the last 2,000 miles have been trouble free. hope this helps||||reg||3
Z||000012||2birds||11-27-1999||11:58 AM||||Here's what I've found out so far...<BR>Checked this morning, fuel pump ran like it's supposed to, but the car won't start.  Pulled a plug and checked for spark, and guess what.  No spark.  I pulled out my ignition module, and my pickup coil has two wires that connect to it that are really corroded and the insulation is cracked.  I'm going to test the module and check on getting a new pickup coil, but of course the dealer parts counters are all closed (Saturday).||||reg||1
Z||000013||Sage||11-27-1999||01:00 PM||||Both the ign.mod. and p/u coil are usually available over the counter at most parts houses.  Last mod. I got came from Autozone was about $25.00 as did the p/u coil around $6.00.  They can test the mod. for you, but usually not the coil.  Sounds like the same problem I had on both an 87GT and 86GT.  In both cases the mod. was the problem and new one the fix.||||reg||1
Z||000014||2birds||11-27-1999||09:27 PM||||The ignition module passed on the tester, so I broke down and bought a pickup coil at Discount Auto.  $7.98.  I'm going to put it in tomorrow and give it a try.  The hold down screws for the module and the connectors for the pickup coil were really corroded, so I'll clean the screws real good before I put it back in.  I just hope I don't destroy my distibutor.  Again, thanks for the ideas, and keep 'em coming!||||reg||1
Z||000015||2birds||11-28-1999||07:45 PM||||Good news.  I replaced the pickup coil and every thing works great now.  The old coil literally fell apart as I removed it from the distributor.  I couldn't find a new o-ring anywhere for the distibutor, but it doesn't leak.  Thanks for all the suggestions.||||reg||1
